al directions (Top-Left). Black needs to develop shape and lines that are not easily blocked.\n2.  **Move Candidates:**\n    -   **H9** (Vertical extension): Forms a vertical line (H8-H9). However, this invites White to play H7, which would simultaneously block Black\'s vertical line and connect White\'s G7 stone (forming G7-H7). This is passive for Black.\n    -   **I8** (Horizontal extension): Forms a horizontal line (H8-I8). Similarly, this invites White to play H7 or G8 to block while building White\'s own connection.\n    -   **I9** (Diagonal extension): This move extends the diagonal line from H8 to the bottom-right. Although the top-left of this line is blocked by G7, the bottom-right is completely open. By playing I9, Black projects influence into the large open space of the board. Unlike H9 or I8, this move does not invite White to play a natural "block-and-connect" move.\n    -   **G9** (Diagonal/Knight\'s shape): Puts pressure on the G column, but can lead to complex contact fighting if White plays G8.\n\n**Reason:**\nThe move **I9** is the most robust standard development. It extends the Black line of stones away from the opponent\'s block (G7) into open territory. This allows Black to look for future "Open 3" or "Open 4" opportunities along the diagonal or in conjunction with neighboring points (like J10 or I8 later), maximizing the flexibility and growth potential of the shape.\n\n**Answer:**\nEvaluation: The move I9 extends the diagonal line from the center stone H8 towards the open bottom-right area. It avoids immediate blocking maneuvers by White (such as H7) that would be effective against vertical or horizontal extensions.\nReason: To build a connected shape with open potential (a diagonal line of 2) and maintain initiative by expanding away from the opponent\'s strong point at G7.\nAnswer: { "col": "I", "row": 9 }'}
